div.p:
 This is considered under four heads (defined in sect. 6): there are at stake 

(1) the dignity and prestige of Rome (sects. 7- 11 ); 

(2) the safety of the allies (sects. 12
							, 13 ); 

(3) the chief revenues of the state (sects. 14- 16 ); 

(4) the investments of the 
										publicani, whose embarrassment would cause a
							financial panic in Rome itself (sects. 17- 19 ).

